     1	# Cost of Operation
     2	
     3	> What does it cost to run a project under `ceo-orchestration`?
     4	> Spoiler: less than running an unstructured Claude Code session
     5	> for the same work, because per-role model dispatch puts the cheap
     6	> models on high-frequency fan-out and saves the expensive model for
     7	> orchestration + critical VETOs.
     8	
     9	## TL;DR — order of magnitude
    10	
    11	A typical session that produces one PR-ready change with a code
    12	review + security check + tests:
    13	
    14	| Configuration | Per session | Per 50 sessions/month | Per 200 sessions/month |
    15	|---------------|-------------|------------------------|-------------------------|
    16	| **All-Opus** baseline (`CEO_MULTIMODEL_ENABLE=0`) | ~$1.23 | ~$62 | ~$246 |
    17	| **Per-role dispatch** (genuine, all native) | ~$1.06 | ~$53 | ~$212 |
    18	| **+ Cache hit** (warm gate-1, ~30% multi-turn savings) | ~$0.75 | ~$37 | ~$148 |
    19	| **Mitigated rail default-on** (ADR-082, actual default) | ~$1.23 | ~$62 | ~$246 |
    20	
    21	Computed at **Opus 4.8 ($5/$25 per Mtok)** — the default-CEO/VETO model
    22	AT THE TIME OF THIS COMPUTATION (2026-06; the current fleet runs the
    23	VETO holders on `claude-fable-5` — see the Gen-5 pricing table above;
    24	opus-5 shares the $5/$25 rate, so the arithmetic below holds for an
    25	opus-5 session). Note the "Mitigated rail default-on" row lands at
    26	**≈ the all-Opus baseline**: under ADR-082, 4 of the 5 canonical archetypes
    27	inherit the Opus CEO model, so the genuine per-role split (~$1.06) is
    28	only realised if you disable mitigation. Numbers are rough. Real usage
    29	varies by skill mix, plan complexity,
    30	and how many debates you trigger. Use the
    31	[`ceo-cost.py`](#measuring-cost) tool to measure your actual spend
    32	on your audit log instead of trusting these estimates.
    33	
    34	## ⚠ Mitigated dispatch — Opus rate inheritance disclosure (audit-v2 C3-P0-03)
    35	
    36	> **PLAN-044 audit-v2 Wave B disclosure.** The "Per-role dispatch"
    37	> row above assumes ADR-052 §Role-to-model distribution applies
    38	> uniformly. **It does not at v1.11.0.** The actual default cost is
    39	> closer to the new "Mitigated rail default-on" row.
    40	
    41	Per **ADR-082** (PLAN-061 default-on, accepted 2026-04-27), only
    42	**1 of the 5 canonical archetypes runs on the native rail** at
    43	default settings: `code-reviewer`. The other 4 (`qa-architect`,
    44	`performance-engineer`, `security-engineer`, `devops`) default
    45	to **mitigated dispatch** — the harness re-routes the Task call
    46	through `subagent_type=general-purpose` with the role's persona
    47	injected via `## SKILL CONTENT`.
    48	
    49	**Cost consequence:** the `general-purpose` sub-agent **inherits
    50	the CEO model** — Opus 4.8 at the time of this computation; on the
    51	current fleet, whatever Gen-5 model the session runs (Fable 5/Opus 5,
    52	same $5-$25+/Mtok tier) — (not the
    53	Sonnet/Haiku that ADR-052 maps these roles to). Every
    54	`qa-architect / performance-engineer / security-engineer / devops`
    55	spawn therefore bills at **Opus rates ($5/$25 per Mtok)**, not
    56	the **$3/$15 Sonnet rates** suggested by the ADR-052 policy table.
    57	
    58	A representative session that would cost ~$1.06 under genuine per-role
    59	dispatch lands at ~$1.23 under the v1.11.0 default because ~75% of the
    60	spawn fan-out routes through Opus by inheritance — and at Opus 4.8 the
    61	Opus/Sonnet gap is small ($5/$25 vs $3/$15), so the inheritance penalty
    62	is now modest (~16%, not the ~60% it was at Opus-4.7 rates).
    63	
    64	### Why ADR-082 ships this default
    65	
    66	The H4 rail anomaly (ADR-080) found that only `code-reviewer`
    67	spawns are reliable on the native rail. Other archetypes empirically
    68	exhibit higher fabrication rates on the native rail in some
    69	release-channel + model combinations. ADR-082 trades cost for
    70	correctness on those 4 archetypes by default.
    71	
    72	### Override — restore native rail to all archetypes
    73	
    74	```bash
    75	# Force native rail for ALL archetypes (full ADR-052 honored).
    76	# Cost reverts to ~$1.06/session; correctness reverts to whatever
    77	# the H4 rail anomaly produces in your release channel.
    78	export CEO_MITIGATION_DISABLE=1
    79	```
    80	
    81	Or per-spawn via the dispatcher CLI:
    82	
    83	```bash
    84	.claude/scripts/inject-agent-context.sh --dispatch=native qa-architect "<task>"
    85	```
    86	
    87	See `docs/CEO-MITIGATION-DISPATCH.md` for the full routing rule
    88	and ADR-082 for the empirical rationale.
    89	
    90	### What `code-reviewer` does
    91	
    92	`code-reviewer` runs natively + at **the VETO-floor model by policy**
    93	(ADR-052; historically Opus 4.8, currently `claude-fable-5` — same
    94	floor mechanism, membership in `VETO_FLOOR_ALLOWED`), not by
    95	inheritance. The cost there is identical
    96	under either rail. Only the other 4 canonicals see the Opus-by-
    97	inheritance surprise.
    98	
    99	## Inputs to the cost model
   100	
   101	### Anthropic public pricing (2025-2026)
   102	
   103	| Model | Input ($/M tokens) | Output ($/M tokens) | vs Opus 4-8 |
   104	|-------|--------------------|----------------------|-------------|
   105	| `claude-fable-5` (current — Mythos-class flagship) | $10.00 | $50.00 | 2.0× |
   106	| `claude-opus-5` (current) | $5.00 | $25.00 | 1.0× |
   107	| `claude-sonnet-5` (current; intro $2/$10 until 2026-08-31, sticker $3/$15) | $2.00 | $10.00 | 0.4× |
   108	| `claude-haiku-4-5` (current) | $1.00 | $5.00 | 0.2× |
   109	| `claude-opus-4-8` (N-1, active) | $5.00 | $25.00 | 1.0× |
   110	| `claude-opus-4-7` (historical — retained for log replay) | $15.00 | $75.00 | 3.0× |
   111	| `claude-sonnet-4-6` (N-1, active) | $3.00 | $15.00 | 0.6× |
   112	| `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001` (dated pin of haiku-4-5) | $1.00 | $5.00 | 0.2× |
   113	
   114	Gen-5 rows added 2026-08-09 (PLAN-169 W2.10 D7, mirroring `ceo-cost.py`).
   115	Live-confirmed 2026-05-29 from https://www.anthropic.com/api (Opus 4.8 = $5/$25, Sonnet 4.6 = $3/$15, Haiku 4.5 = $1/$5). The prior Haiku row ($0.25/$1.25) propagated a stale rate from ADR-052 §Cost magnitude and underpriced Haiku 4x. Adopters with their own contractual
   116	pricing should override the table via `CEO_PRICING_PATH=<json>` —
   117	the `_lib/adapters/live/_cost.py` resolver picks it up.
   118	
   119	### Per-role distribution (ADR-052 default)
   120	
   121	| Agent | Model (at computation; current fleet in parens) | Why |
   122	|-------|-------|-----|
   123	| **CEO orchestrator** (you, the chat session) | Opus 4.8 (now the session model — Fable 5/Opus 5) | Long context, L3+ decisions, debate synthesis |
   124	| **code-reviewer** | Opus 4.8 (now `claude-fable-5`) | Merge VETO — false negative ships a bug |
   125	| **security-engineer** | Opus 4.8 (now `claude-fable-5`) | Auth/crypto VETO — missed attack surface = incident |
   126	| **qa-architect** | Sonnet 4.6 | Edge-case enumeration; bounded work; cost 0.6× vs Opus 4.8 |
   127	| **performance-engineer** | Sonnet 4.6 | Metric analysis; deterministic; cost 0.6× vs Opus 4.8 |
   128	| **devops** | Haiku 4.5 | Config edits + boilerplate; high-freq + low-novelty; cost ~5× cheaper than Opus 4.8 |
   129	
   130	Domain agents (e.g. fintech `financial-correctness-and-math`) inherit
   131	the CEO model unless the project's `team-personas.md` specifies a
   132	`model:` override.
   133	
   134	### Cache behavior
   135	
   136	The gate-1 boot ceremony (CLAUDE.md + PROTOCOL.md + team.md +
   137	frontend-team.md + ceo-orchestration SKILL.md) is **~44,786 tokens**.
   138	Anthropic prompt caching gives ~90% read discount for cached prefix
   139	on subsequent turns within the 5-minute TTL.
   140	
   141	| Scenario | Cost impact |
   142	|----------|-------------|
   143	| First turn of a session (cold) | Full price for gate-1 boot |
   144	| Subsequent turns within 5 min | ~90% savings on cached prefix (the gate-1 boot fits the cache window) |
   145	| Cache miss (TTL expired or gate-1 invalidated) | Full price again |
   146	
   147	**The cache discipline rule** (CLAUDE.md §0): never edit gate-1
   148	files mid-session. Each mid-session edit costs ~44,786 tokens
   149	re-charge on the next turn. See `docs/opus-4-7-operations.md` §2 for
   150	the full discipline.
     1	# CTO evaluation guide — ceo-orchestration
     2	
     3	**Last reviewed:** 2026-08-05 — counts below are gated by `bash .claude/scripts/local/verify-counts.sh`, which fails if any of them drifts from the live tree. This document carries no version literal on purpose; `VERSION` is the single source of truth.
     4	**Audience:** CTO, VP Engineering, Head of Platform, Principal Security.
     5	**Reading time:** 30 minutes if you verify every claim.
     6	**Companion docs:** [`HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md`](./HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md)
     7	(read this first if short on time) · [`threat-model.md`](./threat-model.md)
     8	· [`../SBOM.md`](../SBOM.md).
     9	
    10	Every numeric claim in this document is verifiable by running the command
    11	shown inline against a clean clone at the tagged version. If you can't
    12	reproduce a number, it is a bug in this doc — file it.
    13	
    14	---
    15	
    16	## 1. What this is
    17	
    18	A governance + agent-orchestration framework for Claude Code. It installs
    19	into an existing repo and turns a single-Claude session into a structured
    20	team (CEO, VPs, ICs, staff with veto) executing a Plan→Debate→Execute
    21	protocol, with mechanical hooks enforcing the governance in-band with
    22	every tool invocation.
    23	
    24	- **Runtime:** Python ≥ 3.9 (stdlib-only) + Bash for install.
    25	- **Install target:** any repo with a `.claude/` directory convention.
    26	- **Operational mode:** local workstation. No server. No phone-home.
    27	- **License:** MIT.
    28	
    29	It is **not** a product, not a hosted service, not a Claude Code
    30	replacement. It is a protocol shipped as files.
    31	
    32	---
    33	
    34	## 2. What ships in the box
    35	
    36	Reproduce the table below via the commands in the right column. Counts
    37	refresh every release; if a number drifts, file an issue — that is a
    38	documentation bug.
    39	
    40	| Artifact | Count | Verify |
    41	|---|---|---|
    42	| Python tests collected | ~14,000 | `make test-collect` (or `python3 -m pytest --collect-only -q \| tail -1` — pytest.ini pins the testpath roots) |
    43	| Test files | ~730 | `git ls-files '*test_*.py' '*_test.py' \| wc -l` |
    44	| ADRs shipped | 190 | `ls .claude/adr/ADR-*.md \| wc -l` |
    45	| SPEC/v1 files | 32 (28 `*.schema.md`) | `ls SPEC/v1/*.md \| wc -l` |
    46	| Workflows | 22 | `ls .github/workflows/*.yml \| wc -l` |
    47	| GitHub Actions SHA-pinned refs | every `uses:` pinned | `grep -rEc 'uses: [^#]+@(v[0-9]+\|main\|master\|latest)[[:space:]]*$' .github/workflows/*` — must be 0 everywhere |
    48	| Skills | 166 (42 core + 8 frontend + 116 domain) | `find .claude/skills -name SKILL.md \| wc -l` |
    49	| Hooks | 57 .py on disk; 46 wired into `settings.json` (48 event registrations) | `ls .claude/hooks/*.py \| wc -l` |
    50	| `_lib/` stdlib-only modules | 68 | `ls .claude/hooks/_lib/*.py \| grep -v __init__ \| wc -l` |
    51	| Runtime 3rd-party deps | 0 | see `SBOM.md` §1 |
    52	
    53	Secondary (not strictly reproducible via one-liner, but derivable):
    54	
    55	- **Mutation conformance:** 85 mutation fixtures under
    56	  `tests/formal_verification/mutation_fixtures/`. The contract is the
    57	  expected-kills manifest, not a headline percentage: the harness fails if
    58	  a fixture the manifest expects to die survives. Reproduce:
    59	  `python3 -m pytest tests/formal_verification/ -q`.
    60	- **TLA+ spec coverage:** 4 TLA+ specs published under
    61	  `docs/formal-verification/` (breaker, debate-convergence, plan-lifecycle,
    62	  swarm-coordinator). None of them is model-checked in CI — they are
    63	  specifications, not a verified-system claim (CLAUDE.md §5).
