   175	
   176	## 5. Risk budget (what you are signing up for)
   177	
   178	| Axis | Current state | Mitigation | Residual |
   179	|---|---|---|---|
   180	| Upstream velocity | Bus factor 1 | Fork-friendly; no server dependency | Patches stack locally if Owner unavailable |
   181	| Production signal | 0 external adopters | Dogfood telemetry (`adopter-metrics.py`) + published benchmarks | You may be adopter #1 |
   182	| Platform | macOS + Linux | WSL2 works empirically, no CI gate | Windows-native: not scoped |
   183	| LLM dependency | Anthropic Claude | ADR-032 Gemini adapter stubbed | Vendor lock until cross-model parity |
   184	| Governance drift | Hooks fail-open on infra bugs | `check_canonical_edit.py` sentinel + CODEOWNERS | Intentional: never block operator on hook bug |
   185	| Audit log tamper | HMAC-chained JSONL, shipped (tamper-EVIDENT) | `audit-verify-chain.py` detects forgery / reorder / deletion | Detection, not prevention — the key lives on the same host; pair with a remote OTEL sink (`docs/otel-integration.md`) |
   186	
   187	Read [`HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md`](./HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md) for the full
   188	structural list.
   189	
   190	---
   191	
   192	## 6. What it does well (backed by numbers, not adjectives)
   193	
   194	- **Deterministic governance in-band with tool use.** 46 wired into
   195	  `.claude/settings.json` gate PreToolUse + PostToolUse. Denials are
   196	  structured JSON, not English.
   197	- **Audit trail that survives restart.** Every governance decision
   198	  writes a JSONL line to `~/.claude/projects/<slug>/audit-log.jsonl`.
   199	  Schema versioned (see `SPEC/v1/audit-log.schema.md`, which carries its own
   200	  version history). Replay-friendly.
   201	- **Specified core state machine.** `_breaker.py` modeled in TLA+
   202	  (`docs/formal-verification/breaker.tla`), exercised by the conformance
   203	  harness that owns the 85 mutation fixtures. The spec is not
   204	  model-checked in CI, and this is one component, not the framework —
   205	  see §8 of HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md.
   206	- **Zero runtime third-party deps.** Hooks run with just CPython.
   207	  Install on an air-gapped workstation with Python 3.9+ and you're done.
   208	- **Published evaluation artifacts.** `benchmarks/public/` compares this
   209	  framework against 4 alternatives with reproducible shell blocks.
   210	
   211	---
   212	
   213	## 7. What it will NOT do for you
   214	
   215	- **It will not make your codebase safe.** It makes *Claude sessions*
   216	  against your codebase more structured. Bad code review by humans still
   217	  merges bad code.
   218	- **It will not replace CI.** Hooks run locally. Your `validate.yml`
   219	  still has to pass on `main`.
   220	- **It will not port your prompts.** Skill migration from other
   221	  meta-prompting frameworks (e.g. karpathy-skills, GSD) is manual.
   222	- **It will not run unattended.** There is no autonomous loop. The
   223	  closest surface is the Owner-invoked `/night-mode` posture toggle —
   224	  armed per-session, reversible, and audited — and a human still starts
   225	  and reviews every run.
   226	
   227	---
   228	
   229	## 8. Decision tree
   230	
   231	- **You want production-validated framework with large adopter base:**
   232	  this is not it today — external-adopter validation is explicitly out
   233	  of scope for the current line (HONEST-LIMITATIONS.md §2).
   234	- **You want hosted / SaaS:** not offered. MIT source.
   235	- **You want to evaluate Claude Code governance patterns on a real
   236	  codebase with structured critique and mechanical enforcement:** install
   237	  in a side project for 14 days. Run `.claude/scripts/adopter-metrics.py
   238	  --window 14d`. Report friction.
   239	- **You want to fork and remove the governance you dislike:** encouraged.
   240	  The design is file-based precisely so you can diff and delete.
   241	
