    65	
    66	   - Attestation present + tag exists → the CI red was transient;
    67	     re-run the workflow.
    68	   - Latest version has **no matching tag in this repo** → treat as
    69	     **npm-regression (suspected account compromise)** immediately.
    70	     Do not wait for a second signal.
    71	
    72	## 2. Classify
    73	
    74	Pick one primary class; it determines the containment path.
    75	
    76	| Class | Signals | Typical root cause |
    77	|---|---|---|
    78	| **action-compromise** | Upstream tag now dereferences to a different SHA than our pin's `# tag` comment claims; or public advisory about the action | Upstream action repo/tag hijacked. NOTE: our SHA pin means we are NOT executing the new code — the drift is a signal about the upstream ecosystem, and a prompt to re-review before any future bump |
    79	| **npm-regression** | Provenance attestation missing on latest; `npm audit signatures` fails; a published version with no matching repo tag | Worst case: npm account/token compromise and an out-of-band publish. Milder: a publish pipeline change dropped `--provenance` |
    80	| **our-drift** | Policy/format violation introduced by a commit in this repo | A workflow merged without SHA pin, with `pull_request_target`, or with an unguarded fork-reachable secret |
    81	
    82	Severity mapping (ladder defined in `INCIDENT-RESPONSE.md`): a published
    83	rogue npm version is SEV-1; action-compromise where our pin predates the
    84	hijack is SEV-3 (we are pinned; no exposure) unless a workflow already
    85	bumped to the hijacked SHA; our-drift is SEV-3 unless a secret was
    86	actually reachable from a fork PR, then treat as SEV-2 and rotate.
    87	
    88	## 3. Contain
    89	
    90	### 3a. action-compromise
   160	3. **If no user exposure** (pinned action drift, our-drift caught by
   161	   the watch before merge to a release): a closing comment on the
   162	   pinned issue + post-mortem file `docs/incidents/YYYY-MM-DD-<slug>.md`
   163	   (template in `INCIDENT-RESPONSE.md`) is sufficient. No advisory for
   164	   non-exposures — do not inflate.
   165	4. Close the pinned issue only when the watch runs green again
   166	   (re-trigger via `workflow_dispatch` — do not wait for Monday).
   167	
   168	---
   169	
   170	## Honest limits of the watch
   171	
   172	- **Axis (a)** proves our pins are SHAs and our workflow policy holds;
   173	  the drift leg only fires for pins that carry a `# tag` comment, and
   174	  it compares refs, not code. It cannot detect a compromised action
   175	  whose tag was hijacked *before* we first pinned it.
   176	- **Axis (b)** proves the registry still serves a provenance
   177	  attestation and signatures verify. It does NOT rebuild the package or
   178	  diff the tarball against this repo, and it cannot stop a compromised
   179	  account publishing a *new* version with valid provenance from a
   180	  compromised workflow — the tag-correspondence check is only a partial
   181	  tripwire for that. `npm audit signatures` on a zero-dependency
   182	  package verifies exactly one package; it adds no dependency-tree
   183	  coverage because there is no tree.
   184	- The watch runs weekly. The exposure window between incident and
   185	  detection can be up to 7 days; for anything time-critical, trigger it
   186	  manually (`workflow_dispatch`).
