    64	
    65	**Message:** `GOVERNANCE: anti-CEO-overhead ...`
    66	
    67	**Cause:** `check_anti_ceo_overhead.py` fires when the CEO does work
    68	itself that should be delegated (one of the P1-P5 predicates — e.g.
    69	writing bulk code inline instead of spawning a specialist).
    70	
    71	**Fix:** delegate via `/spawn`. If the action is genuinely correct and
    72	you accept the overhead, what the mechanism delivers today is:
    73	
    74	- **Bash commands:** prefix the single command with
    75	  `CEO_OVERHEAD_ACK=1 ` (e.g. `CEO_OVERHEAD_ACK=1 grep …`) and it
    76	  proceeds.
    77	- **Edit/Write tools:** there is NO per-action override channel.
    78	  The predicates evaluate a **5-minute sliding window** of classified
    79	  actions — stop the grep/read burst and retry the edit after the
    80	  window drains.
    81	- **Session-wide:** exporting `CEO_OVERHEAD_ACK=1` in the environment
    82	  that launches the session disables blocking for the whole session
    83	  (the override is audited). Use sparingly.
    84	
    65	**Mensagem:** `GOVERNANCE: anti-CEO-overhead ...`
    66	
    67	**Causa:** `check_anti_ceo_overhead.py` dispara quando o CEO faz por
    68	conta própria um trabalho que deveria ser delegado (um dos predicados
    69	P1-P5 — ex.: escrever código em massa inline em vez de spawnar um
    70	especialista).
    71	
    72	**Fix:** delegue via `/spawn`. Se a ação está genuinamente correta e
    73	você aceita o overhead, o que o mecanismo entrega hoje é:
    74	
    75	- **Comandos Bash:** prefixe o comando único com
    76	  `CEO_OVERHEAD_ACK=1 ` (ex.: `CEO_OVERHEAD_ACK=1 grep …`) e ele
